  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2033}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point.",
